  <%# Deliberately OUTSIDE the @revealed_token gate above: the instructions have to outlive the
      one-shot flash, or a user who reloads the page has no way to learn what the key is for. %>
  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Connect this repository", id: "connect") do %>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="space-y-4 lg:col-span-2" data-controller="copy-text">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Endpoint</p>
          <p class="font-mono text-sm break-all text-app-content">GET <%= api_v1_repository_url %></p>
        </div>

        <div class="space-y-2">
          <p class="section-label text-app-content-secondary">Try it</p>
          <%# Gated on the repository actually having a key. `<token>` has no value to stand for
              until one exists, so before that this rendered a command whose only possible outcome
              was a 401 — and told the reader to substitute a key they had never minted. %>
          <% if has_api_keys %>
            <%= render UI::CopyableCodeComponent.new do %>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;" <%= api_v1_repository_url %><% end %>
            <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
              <code>&lt;token&gt;</code> is a placeholder — substitute the key you copied when you minted
              it. SpecGuard cannot show an existing key again; regenerate it to mint a replacement.
            </p>
          <% elsif repository_policy.can?(:keys_manage) %>
            <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
              This repository has no API key yet, and the endpoint answers
              <code>401 {"error":"unauthorized"}</code> without one. Mint a key in
              <a href="#api-keys" class="underline">API keys</a> below — the command to run is shown
              with the key already in it, once.
            </p>
          <% else %>
            <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
              This repository has no API key yet, and the endpoint answers
              <code>401 {"error":"unauthorized"}</code> without one. Ask
              <%= @repository.user.display_name %> to mint one.
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4 lg:col-span-1">
        <%# == Four states, because "a request arrived", "a run was accepted" and "the token that
            arrived still exists" are three questions
            `@last_api_request_at` is `api_keys.last_used_at`, and `authenticate_api_key!` stamps
            that on the way IN — before the payload is looked at. So it answers "did anything
            authenticate", and that is the only question it can answer.

            Read alone it produced the product's own Vacuous Green: a repository whose every run
            was refused for its payload rendered "Connected", in success tone, with a hint saying
            the last request was two minutes ago. CI green, dashboard green, and a pipeline 100%
            failing. The refusal is now recorded (`IngestRejection`), so the stat can ask the
            second question too, and the refusing case is checked FIRST — it is a strictly more
            specific state than "something authenticated", and it implies it, since a delivery
            cannot be refused for its payload without having authenticated.

            `:error` rather than `:warning`, and above "Not connected yet" in severity on purpose:
            a repository that has never connected has nothing broken, while this one has a CI
            pipeline doing work and throwing all of it away. `RejectedIngests#refusing?` carries
            the rule — the newest refusal is newer than the newest accepted run — and both of its
            stated bounds.

            The THIRD question is the one a rotation opens. `regenerate!` retires a token with no
            grace window and deliberately does not touch `last_used_at` (SPGD-352: it is the key's
            history), so between the rotation and the replacement reaching CI this stat was
            reporting an age stamped by a credential that no longer exists — success tone over a
            pipeline 401ing on every delivery. `@last_live_api_request_at` is the same figure
            restricted to keys still carrying the token that stamped them, so "Connected" now
            reports an age that belongs to something that exists, and the case where every such key
            has been rotated gets its own branch instead of borrowing that one's tone.

            Between `refusing?` and "Not connected yet" on both their reasonings: a delivery that is
            being refused has authenticated, so it is the more specific state and stays first; and
            this repository HAS connected, so it must not fall through to the branch for one that
            never has. `:warning` and not `:error` because nothing is being thrown away and nothing
            is provably broken — the replacement may have been minted a minute ago — but something
            has to happen off this page before CI works again, and the hint names it. %>
        <% if @rejected_ingests.refusing? %>
          <%= render UI::StatComponent.new(
                label: "Connection",
                value: "Deliveries refused",
                hint: "The key works — the payload did not. Last refused " \
                      "#{time_ago_in_words(@rejected_ingests.last_rejection_at)} ago; " \
                      "see Rejected deliveries below.",
                tone: :error
              ) %>
        <% elsif @last_live_api_request_at.present? %>
          <%= render UI::StatComponent.new(
                label: "Connection",
                value: "Connected",
                hint: "Last request #{time_ago_in_words(@last_live_api_request_at)} ago.",
                tone: :success
              ) %>
        <% elsif @last_api_request_at.present? %>
          <%# WHICH ROTATION TO DATE, once this is a SET rather than a key. Every stranded key
              satisfies the rule against ITS OWN rotation — its use is not newer than its own
              `rotated_at` — so the only claim true of all of them at once is that none has
              authenticated since it was regenerated. No single date carries that:

              * the NEWEST rotation makes "nothing has authenticated since <date>" true, and it is
                the most REASSURING true reading available, on the one stat this whole change
                exists to stop reading reassuringly. A nightly rotated five days ago and never
                updated, beside a main rotated a minute ago, would date the reader's five-day-dead
                pipeline at one minute.
              * the OLDEST rotation in that same sentence is plainly FALSE whenever a second key
                authenticated after the first key's rotation — which is the ordinary mixed case.

              So the SENTENCE changes shape with the set instead of one date quietly changing
              meaning: one key names its own rotation; several report how many and date the OLDEST,
              which is how long something here has been stranded and points at the pipeline that
              has been down longest. Both readings are true of every key in the set.

              `rotations` cannot be empty on this branch: `@last_api_request_at` is present and
              `@last_live_api_request_at` is not, so the key holding that use is stranded, and a
              stranded key has a `rotated_at` by `rotated_and_unused?`'s first clause. %>
          <% rotations = @rotated_unused_api_keys.filter_map(&:rotated_at).sort %>
          <%= render UI::StatComponent.new(
                label: "Connection",
                value: "Key rotated, not yet in use",
                hint: if rotations.one?
                        "Nothing has authenticated since the key was regenerated " \
                        "#{time_ago_in_words(rotations.first)} ago — the replacement token has " \
                        "not reached CI. Update it wherever the old one was stored."
                      else
                        "#{rotations.size} keys have been regenerated with nothing authenticating " \
                        "since; the oldest was regenerated #{time_ago_in_words(rotations.first)} " \
                        "ago — the replacement tokens have not reached CI. Update them wherever " \
                        "the old ones were stored."
                      end,
                tone: :warning
              ) %>
        <% else %>
          <%= render UI::StatComponent.new(
                label: "Connection",
                value: "Not connected yet",
                hint: "No API key has been used.",
                tone: :warning
              ) %>
        <% end %>

        <%# The two families genuinely differ, and this said so in a way that was true of one of
            them. "A rejected request never records a use" is true for a 401 — `render_unauthorized`
            returns three lines BEFORE the stamp — and false for a 400, where authentication
            already succeeded and the stamp had already fired. The sentence taught the reader to
            treat a recorded use as proof the pipeline works, which is precisely the inference a
            refused payload defeats. It now names which family does which rather than dropping the
            distinction.

            The "never" rule was also the one heuristic this panel handed the reader, and it is the
            one that cannot fire on the failure it is most needed for: a rotated key is never at
            "never" — it inherits its predecessor's timestamp — so a reader applying the rule as
            written concludes a 401ing pipeline is healthy. The rotation sentence is what makes the
            rule cover the state the panel now detects. %>
        <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :neutral) do %>
          A wrong or revoked key gets <code>401 {"error":"unauthorized"}</code> and records no use at
          all — authenticating is what stamps the key — so a key stuck at "never" means the request
          never authenticated, or was never sent at all. Regenerating a key retires its token
          immediately and leaves that stamp standing, so a rotated key reads "not used since
          rotation" rather than "never" until the replacement reaches CI — until then every delivery
          gets the same 401. A request that authenticates and is then refused for its
          <em>payload</em> (<code>400 {"error":"bad_request"}</code>) <em>does</em> record a use, so
          a recent use is not on its own proof that a run was stored. Those refusals are listed in
          <a href="#rejected-ingests" class="underline">Rejected deliveries</a> below.
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
